# Evaluation Criteria (what reviewers will actually test)

Five dimensions, 20% each. Bonus items don't change the core score but are noted positively.
Reviewers WILL phone the number and hit the API. Build for those calls, not for a demo in your head.

## Priority order when time is short
1. A working end-to-end call that saves a record (Working System). If this fails, nothing else counts.
2. Confirmation read-back, corrections, field-specific re-prompts (Conversational Quality + Edge Cases).
3. API correctness: envelope, status codes, validation, soft delete (Technical Architecture).
4. README + commented system prompt (Code Quality & Docs).
5. Bonus, in this order: duplicate detection, call transcripts, dashboard, tests, appointments, Spanish.

Rules:
- Never start a bonus item while a core item is broken.
- Deploy early (first hour) and re-test with a real call after every meaningful change.
- A partial but working submission beats an ambitious broken one. Unfinished work goes in README "Next Steps".

---

## 1. Working System (20%)
Reviewers check:
- Can we call the number and complete a registration?
- Is the data persisted and retrievable via the API?
- Does a second call work without data loss?

We must:
- Keep the Railway service always on (no sleeping instance, no cold start on the first call).
- Use Supabase (external DB), so redeploys never wipe data.
- Have `GET /health` that checks DB connectivity and reports the Gemini key pool status (keys masked).

Verify: make 2 real calls back to back, then `GET /patients` and confirm both records. Redeploy, check again.

## 2. Conversational Quality (20%)
Reviewers check:
- Sounds natural, not robotic or scripted.
- Handles corrections gracefully ("Actually, my last name is spelled D-A-V-I-S, not D-A-V-I-E-S").
- Confirms information before saving.
- Handles interruptions and out-of-order answers.

We must:
- Keep agent turns to 1-2 sentences, vary acknowledgments, no lists or symbols in speech.
- Accept multiple fields in one utterance and never re-ask something already given.
- Read back all fields (spell the last name, phone digit by digit) before any save.
- Rely on Vapi barge-in for interruptions and tell the LLM not to restart its sentence after one.
- Stream LLM output and keep time-to-first-token low. Lag feels robotic even if the words are good.

## 3. Technical Architecture (20%)
Reviewers check:
- Clear separation: telephony vs LLM logic vs data layer vs API.
- Well-designed schema with proper types and constraints.
- RESTful, validated endpoints.
- Thoughtful, documented prompt engineering.

We must:
- Keep folders strictly layered (see `02-tech-stack.mdc`). Vapi payload parsing lives only in the telephony adapter.
- One service layer shared by REST routes and voice tools.
- Postgres constraints (CHECK, NOT NULL, enum, partial indexes) mirror Pydantic validation.
- System prompt lives in code (`app/llm/prompt.py`), section-commented, and is explained in the README.

## 4. Code Quality & Documentation (20%)
Reviewers check:
- Organized, readable, consistent code.
- README complete and accurate.
- Trade-offs and limitations documented.
- LLM prompt/system message included and commented.

We must:
- Type hints everywhere, small functions, docstrings on public functions, no dead code, no print statements.
- README sections: Overview, Live demo (phone + API URL), Architecture diagram, Tech stack + why, Setup, Env vars,
  API reference, Prompt design, Testing, Known limitations & trade-offs, Next Steps.
- `.env.example` listing every variable with a comment.

## 5. Edge Cases & Resilience (20%)
Reviewers check:
- Invalid date of birth?
- Telephony connection drops mid-call?
- Database write fails: error or silence?
- Caller wants to start over mid-conversation?

We must:
- Inject today's date into the prompt so the LLM catches future DOBs, and enforce it again server-side.
- Save only after confirmation, so a dropped call never creates a half-filled patient. The end-of-call report
  still logs the call as `incomplete` with the transcript.
- On DB failure: tool returns a structured error, agent apologizes, retries once, then tells the caller clearly.
  Log the full payload so no data is silently lost. Never claim success unless the tool returned success.
- On LLM failure: rotate keys, fall back to a backup model, and as a last resort stream a spoken
  "sorry, give me a moment" line. Never dead air.
- "Start over": confirm, wipe collected info, restart from the name. Do not hang up.

